  "deviations": [
    {
      "summary": "Trials ran on development runtime 25.0.4 rather than the pinned Mojang 25.0.1; setting RAVEL_SPIKE_JAVA to the pinned runtime removes the difference. Every gate carries it, so the figures are comparable to each other and none is a measurement of the runtime players use.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "Boundary-traffic figures are computed from each trial's known signature rather than counted by an instrument, yet are recorded as measured with a collector identity. The arithmetic is correct and no conclusion rests on it.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "Retained RSS and bootstrap peak RSS come from one /proc read taken at the ready marker before any measured invocation, so they are byte-identical in every ADG-1, ADG-2 and ADG-4 result and measure no workload-retained memory.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "ADG-4 describes one flow under three owners, but the three models differ by two Java field writes and one addition, so argument size is confounded with a small difference in work done.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "Seven of twenty results record a trial.revision.id that cannot be reconstructed as described: six ADG-1 results name a predecessor of their own change, and the ADG-5 result names a change above the one holding it. The checked half, trial.revision.changeId, is correct on all twenty and is recomputed by ravel:verify-spikes. Nothing reads the commit field.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "ADG-5 records a stock launch as three substring tests over a redacted log. Nothing verifies the launched version, the launcher identity, that the arguments sat on the game command line, or that the client reached a world or exited cleanly.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "schemas/package.schema.json still requires per-target mod artifacts, which SPEC 3.4 no longer promises. It is disclosed there by name as an inherited assumption and is owned by M3.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "The cold audit returned FAIL with ten findings. All ten were resolved and none has been re-audited, so the last cold verdict recorded against this evidence is FAIL.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    }
  ],
  "risks": [
    {
      "summary": "The selected native binding has never run in the real game. The observed ADG-5 launch used ADG-1's Java-callback variant, so the exported-symbol path is unexercised outside the spike harness. M2-W5 owns closing this.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "The measured hot path carries no panic guard, and the veto-by-throwing hook shape named first in hooks.toml was never measured. 10.02 ns is the unguarded cost.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "ADG-2 retains the dynamic library as a live M3 candidate although it needs two maintained unsafe calls, which the M1 gate failure actions otherwise reject. Selecting it requires a SPEC 3.3 amendment and a safety-boundary row.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    },
    {
      "summary": "The linked candidate's mod-to-mod path is unguarded and crosses extern C, so a panic there aborts exactly as the dynamic library does. Guarding it costs something this gate did not measure.",
      "classification": "non-blocking"
    }
  ],
